<description>
Move the single marker to the endpoint reached by sliding along the obstacle contour.

Input structure:
- A dominant background color fills the canvas.
- One single-pixel marker sits away from a connected obstacle region of the
  remaining color.
- The marker has exactly one horizontal or vertical sightline to the obstacle.

Rule:
- Find the last background pixel before the obstacle along that sightline.
- From that pixel, try the two diagonal turns that continue around the
  obstacle boundary.
- Move the marker to the endpoint of the turn that remains 4-adjacent to the
  obstacle for the longest distance.

Key insight:
The marker is not placed by distance to the obstacle; it first points to the
obstacle, then follows the visible outside contour until that contour segment ends.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "single marker pixel",
  "path": "cardinal sightline from the marker to the obstacle region, then diagonal slide along the 4-adjacent outside contour",
  "dst": "endpoint of the longer contour-following diagonal turn from the last background pixel before the obstacle",
  "trajectory": "move only the marker to dst",
  "transform": "old marker pixel becomes background; keep the obstacle and all other background pixels unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 6-16 rows x 6-16 cols
  colors: exactly 3 -- background (most frequent), obstacle, marker
  background: fills all non-obstacle/marker cells [observed: always 7]
  obstacle:
    shape: solid connected region, 6-33 pixels, 3-8 rows x 3-8 cols
    edges: each row fully filled between its leftmost and rightmost obstacle col
    staircase_edge: at least one edge steps by exactly 1 cell per row/col (the contour side)
    flat_edge: at least one axis-aligned edge is constant (straight wall) [observed]
  marker:
    single pixel, unique color, not touching obstacle (gap >= 1 along sightline)
    position: on grid edge [observed]; exactly one cardinal sightline to obstacle through bg only
  sightline:
    gap: 2-5 bg cells between marker and first obstacle pixel along the one clear cardinal direction
    no other cardinal direction from marker may reach obstacle through pure bg
  contour_walk:
    from last bg cell before obstacle along sightline, try two diagonal turns (sightline + perpendicular)
    one turn must be strictly longer than the other (or both 0); walk length 0-4
    each diagonal cell must be bg AND 4-adjacent to obstacle (ensured by staircase edge)
  output: marker removed from original pos (becomes bg), placed at contour walk endpoint

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first (build settled state, then place marker)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size. Choose obstacle color and bg color (distinct).
    2. Build obstacle with a staircase edge -- e.g. start from a flat base row/col, then add rows with boundary shifting by +1 or 0 per step to form stepped profile. Fill each row solidly.
    3. Choose which staircase edge to use as contour. Pick a point on that edge as the destination (contour endpoint). Pick contour walk length L (1-5).
    4. Walk L diagonal steps back from destination along the staircase to find contour_start. Verify each step is bg and 4-adj to obstacle; verify the opposite diagonal turn is shorter.
    5. From contour_start, extend the sightline direction backward by gap (2-5) cells to place the marker. Verify no other cardinal sightline from marker reaches obstacle.
    6. Choose marker color (distinct from bg and obstacle). Place marker at computed position. Verify marker is not 4-adjacent to obstacle. Output = marker at destination.
```
