139:     Emit diagonal beams from framed 2x2 blocks through frame gaps; beams interact on collision.
140: 
141:     Input structure:
142:     - bg = most common color. Wall color (e.g. green) forms 4x4 frames around 2x2 monochrome blocks.
143:     - Each frame has one or more gap pixels where the wall is missing.
144: 
145:     Rule:
146:     - Each gap emits a beam of the inner block's color, traveling diagonally outward (direction set by gap position relative to the block).
147:     - Beams paint every cell they cross until they hit the grid edge or collide.
148:     - 90° collision: both beams turn 45° toward their bisector direction; the intersection cell is left blank (1-pixel gap).
149:     - 45° collision: beams merge into a zigzag (alternating cardinal + diagonal steps) with no gap at the meeting point.
150: 
151:     Key insight: Two distinct collision rules — 90° produces a gap and single-pixel diagonal lines; 45° produces no gap and thicker zigzag bands.
